The construction these programs implement (2-loops, slices, the selection, the completion, the transport and the
closed trails) is Jay Pantone's, https://github.com/jaypantone/superperm-upper-43-80 (Apache License 2.0). No file
of his repository is included. gen14.c can also write the order of William Echols's 2-lift (recipe
"superperm-2lift/1"); it was written from its author's own definitions, and no code of Echols's repository is used.

The example containers in words/10 to words/14 hold data of jhalfar/superpermutations (Apache License 2.0): the
selection on 8 symbols, which is derived from Pantone's tools/construction-input.txt (untransport.py, twice), the
differences of the published selections against its transports, the block solutions, the n = 11 connection files
and the plans of the published words. Credits for those words, as in the NOTICE of jhalfar/superpermutations: the
construction is Pantone's; the structure of the n = 10 word and the order of its groups were read off rumstd's
n = 10 word; the n = 12 and n = 13 words use two kinds of move that others found, re-opening a block of closed
trails (William Echols) and moving one closed trail and choosing the cut at which it is opened (Theo H.); publishing
an n = 14 word as a generator and a plan follows William Echols's 2-lift recipe.
